Z||000000||86GT-FstBk||09-04-2000||02:16 PM||||I know that NGK plugs are the choice plug for the Fiero but which one is the best for the buck.  The $1.59 regular or the Platinum $5.99 one.<BR>Hey thanks for all of my dumb questions but I'm just trying to get educated about this great vehicle that requires patience and TLC.<P>Brian||||reg||5
Z||000001||stimpy||09-04-2000||02:55 PM||||Uh-oh, not spark plugs again... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I use platinum plugs for the reported longevity, that's it.  If they perform any better then regular, well, that's just a dandy little bonus.  I have no way of knowing if they perform any better, I am just extremely lazy and don't like to change plugs every 10,000 miles or so.||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||09-04-2000||03:02 PM||||Oh Lord...not trying to be rude, but for spark plug info, check the archives. This thing got way outta hand last time....all the info on plugs you need will be in the archives.||||reg||11
Z||000003||Old Lar||09-04-2000||08:48 PM||||Come on guys the sparkplug flame war was the most entertainment around.<P>As we all know the Fiero front plugs are the  most difficult to get to.  A long life platinum plug may be the answer, once its in it'll be good for a long time.  The down side:  once it in for a long time the plug may get corroded never to come out in any easy manner.<P>I read of the 100000 mile Fiero with original equipment plugs still in the car because they are a pain to get at and were never changed.<P>Not to start the war up again, go with a good quality plug.  Let your pocket be your guide.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierosound||09-04-2000||10:05 PM||||I just bought a set of Torque Masters for my 3.4L install (not completed yet).  I bought these based on a recommendation of a fellow Fiero owner who had a high C.R. 3.4L engine (basically, I'm following his build) as follows...<BR>-------------------------------------------<P>&gt;&gt; Also, I wanted to ask, what spark plugs did you wind up using.<P>&gt;Started out with standard AC Delco 2.8 replacements. Had an intermittent ping lugging the motor in 4th or 5th (w/ premium gas) till I backed off timing 2*. <P>A few months later I got magnecor wires, <A HREF="http://www.magnecor.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.magnecor.com/</A> <P>torquemaster plugs (for 88 Fiero 2.8) <A HREF="http://www.torquemaster.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.torquemaster.com/</A> <P>& Crane HI6 ignition <A HREF="http://cranecams.com/FireBall.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://cranecams.com/FireBall.htm</A> <P>Now I can set the timing wherever I want :-)<BR>-------------------------------------------<BR>The plugs are patented and have a claimed life of 200,000+ miles - basically the last plug you'll ever need!  I should be driving  with them by next weekend.||||reg||10
Z||000005||1FST2M6||09-04-2000||11:48 PM||||i had NGK UR5s in my 86 SE with nitrous, MSD 6btm (changed to 6AL for resale) and never had a problem with them... have a set for my GT when it gets back from the paint shop...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||09-05-2000||05:21 AM||||I'm using NGK V-powers in my GT right now.  No problems at all.. <P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
